    "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8001-000000000014": {
      "name": "Four counts of the same thing",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "fig-reflight.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**No count of this flood is final, and the counts do not agree with each other.**\n\nFour organisations counted destroyed buildings. They got\n**2,813**, **5,048**, **1,641** and **677**.\nThey covered different ground, used different methods, and did not mean the same\nthing by destroyed. None of them is the number.\n\n![The same ground graded twice, two days apart](attachment:fig-reflight.png)\n\nCopernicus graded one area on 29 August, then looked again on 31 August. Nothing\non the ground changed in between. The second look found\n**653 more buildings\ndestroyed** than the first.\n\nIt has since re-graded a second area and found\n**105 more**. Both times the number went up, and only\nup.\n\nThe dead are counted the same way. Between 1 and 5 September, Chitwan's toll\ngrew by about a tenth, from **321 to 360**. Rasuwa's grew\nalmost six times, from **27 to 160**. Those two counts\ncome from different bodies, so the change is worth more than the difference.\nBut the mountains are the hard place to count.\n\nEarly counts are too low. So are early maps. The volunteer record stops at\n**km 78**. That is where one mapping project ended and the next\none began. It is not where the flood ended. The water ran on for another 130 km\npast the last red dot.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "52%",
          "title": "Four counts of the same thing"
        },
        "extent": [
          9430547.08159633,
          3217447.3413577504,
          9529588.965596877,
          3290411.7080283663
        ],
