    "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8001-000000000017": {
      "name": "Radar, through the cloud",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "fig-radar-change.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Every camera picture in this map has a hole in it. Radar can see through cloud.**\n\n![Backscatter change, 16 to 28 August](attachment:fig-radar-change.png)\n\nThis is two Sentinel-1 radar passes of the same orbit, twelve days apart, one\nsubtracted from the other. The angle is the same both times. So the shape of the\nland cancels out, and what is left is change on the ground. Smooth surfaces\nbounce the radar away and come out dark. Standing water, wet mud and fresh sand\nare all smoother than what was there before. So a drop in the radar signal means\nthe surface got smoother. It does not say what made it smoother.\n\nAcross the whole box, **4 per cent** of pixels dropped by more than 3 dB. Inside\nUNOSAT's scar, **29 per cent**. Inside the lower lake, **57 per cent**.\n\nThe upper lake is the exception, at 23 per cent, and its middle value barely\nmoves. The camera pictures say that outline filled with something. The radar\nshows the same ground barely changing. This map will not decide between them.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "Radar, through the cloud"
        },
        "extent": [
          9509157.575592786,
          3280822.811478664,
          9527922.503637541,
          3294646.973894613
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
