    "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8001-000000000019": {
      "name": "What could still happen",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "map",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Both lakes sit above the valley the flood has just been through.**\n\nOn 27 August, China's Ministry of Water Resources put one of them at\n**1.5 to 2 million m\u00b3**. It expected 3 million more within three days.\n\nWhat happened next is reported three different ways. Rasuwa officials said on\n28 August that the lake had started to overflow. The Kathmandu Post reported the\nsame day that the Army had told the district officer it had broken through. The\nStimson Center, reading Planet images, wrote that both lakes were still growing.\n\nStimson were more worried about the slope than about the lakes. They warned that\nsmaller slides could start again if it rained hard in the days after. ICIMOD has\nasked for monitoring across the region, because this one started on the Nepali\nside, outside the Chinese early-warning area.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "45%",
          "title": "What could still happen"
        },
        "extent": [
          9497027.86461365,
          3274815.215320033,
          9529699.501972793,
          3298884.480076636
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
          {
            "opacity": 0.25,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
            "visible": true
          },
          {
            "opacity": 1.0,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
            "visible": false
          },
          {
