    "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8001-000000000020": {
      "name": "Sources",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Every number here is either measured from the data in this folder, or quoted\nfrom a named source, with the time it was published.**\n\n**The people and the timeline**\n\n[NDRRMA Situation Report #01](https://ndrrma.gov.np/mediafiles/rasuwa/Rasuwa_Flood_SitRep_Temp_ENG_01_01092026.pdf), 1 September 2026, 09:00.\nThe cut-off villages, the bridges, the schools, the health posts, the arrival\ntimes and the account of the cause all come from it. It is the last situation\nreport NDRRMA has published in English.\n\nThose numbers changed daily, and the later ones here are reported rather than\nmeasured. [The Kathmandu Post, 5 September 2026](https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/09/05/nepal-flood-death-toll-reaches-1-342),\ncarrying the Nepal Police district-by-district count of 8:00 that morning. [The\nKathmandu Post, 7 September 2026](https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/09/07/nepal-flood-death-toll-rises-to-1-355-nearly-5-000-unaccounted-for),\ncarrying NDRRMA's count of 16:20 that day. [BIPAD](https://bipadportal.gov.np/)\ncarries the current ones.\n\n**The damage mapping**\n\n- [Humanitarian OpenStreetMap Team](https://data.humdata.org/dataset/hot_flood_npl). Flood extent, destroyed features, bridges, schools. ODbL 1.0.\n- [Copernicus EMS, EMSR927](https://data.humdata.org/dataset/npl-flood-emsr927). Damage grading. \u00a9 2026 European Union.\n- [UNOSAT, FL20260826NPL](https://data.humdata.org/dataset/mudflow-rockflow-impact-assessment-in-rasuwa-nuwakot-districts-bagmati-province-nepal-as-o). Flood extent, detachment zone, barrier lakes.\n\n**The barrier lakes**\n\n- Costa, J. E. and Schuster, R. L., 1988, [The formation and failure of natural dams](https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70015581), Geological Society of America Bulletin, v. 100, p. 1054-1068. The 73-dam longevity figures, and the warning that they only describe dams that broke.\n- [Al Jazeera, 28 August 2026](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/28/is-nepal-facing-another-devastating-flood-from-a-new-lake). The Chinese volume estimate, the overflow report, and the quotation.\n- [The Kathmandu Post, 28 August 2026](https://kathmandupost.com/national/2026/08/28/barrier-lake-near-china-border-breaches-raising-fresh-flood-threat-in-nepal). The breach report.\n- [Stimson Center, 72 hours later](https://www.stimson.org/2026/a-cascading-disaster-on-the-china-nepal-border-what-to-know-about-the-august-2026-rasuwa-flood/). Both lakes growing, and the risk of the slope moving again.\n- [ICIMOD](https://www.icimod.org/kyirong-rasuwa-flood-2026-nepal-china-border/). The cause, and the case for cross-border monitoring.\n\nThose five are news reports and published research, not measurements. They are\nquoted that way. Where they disagree, the story says so instead of picking one.\n\nThe water index is NDWI: (green - near infrared) / (green + near infrared). It\nwas sampled inside the UNOSAT outlines, on the same Sentinel-2 pictures the\nfigure draws. `METHODS.md` has the numbers, and what they do not settle.\n\n**The imagery**\n\nSentinel-2, 10 m, openly licensed. Every pass after the flood is 70-90% cloud.\nSo each before-and-after is built from the clear pixels of several passes. Ground\nthat never got a clear look is left as a gap.\n\nThe agencies worked from much sharper pictures: Pl\u00e9iades Neo and WorldView-3 at\n0.3 m, plus CARTOSAT-3 and SkySat. Those are commercial and restricted. The\noutlines drawn from them can be shared. The images cannot.\n\nThe radar is Sentinel-1 RTC gamma0 from the Microsoft Planetary Computer, one\nmatched pair on relative orbit 85. It is the only picture here with no cloud in\nit. It is also the only one that shows a difference rather than a photograph.\n\n**The channel and the reaches**\n\nMeasured from terrain tiles for [an earlier map of this\nflood](https://github.com/arjxn-py/nepal-flood-26-aug). That map was made in the\nweek before any of the damage data existed.\n\nThe damage mapping on this page was taken from HDX on **8 September 2026**. It\nis revised daily, so the counts here are a reading of it on that date, not a\nfinal figure.\n\n## Explore the map yourself\n\n**Everything this page showed you is still in the file, and you can open it\n[here](https://notebook.link/@arjxn-py/nffr).** The data it reads, and the\nmethods behind it, are in\n[the repository](https://github.com/arjxn-py/nepal-flood-26-aug).\n\n**Ask a feature where it came from.** Turn on the identify tool in the toolbar\nand click any dot, line or patch. Every feature carries its source, the link to\nthat source, its licence and the date it was fetched.\n\n**Turn layers on and off.** The story only ever showed a few at a time. The\nlayer panel has all of them, including the ones it never put on screen.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "Sources"
        },
        "extent": [
          9360896.486414863,
          3185971.5452115457,
          9533338.422228033,
          3313009.8862228445
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
          {
            "opacity": 0.25,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
            "visible": true
          },
          {
            "opacity": 1.0,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
            "visible": true
          },
          {
            "opacity": 1.0,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000004",
            "visible": false
          },
          {
            "opacity": 1.0,
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000005",
            "visible": false
          },
